tal":null,"votes":null,"evs":{"monroe":11}},"TN":{"ev":8,"method":"popular","total":null,"votes":null,"evs":{"monroe":8}},"VT":{"ev":8,"method":"legislature","total":null,"votes":null,"evs":{"monroe":8}},"VA":{"ev":25,"method":"popular","total":6863,"votes":{"monroe":6859,"unpledged":4},"evs":{"monroe":25}}},"notes":["Indiana became a state on December 11, 1816, after the electors had met. An objection to counting its 3 electoral votes was postponed indefinitely, and they were counted.","Four electors did not vote: 1 in Delaware and the 3 Federalist electors chosen in Maryland’s 1st, 8th and 9th districts.","For vice president, Daniel D. Tompkins was elected with 183 electoral votes. The Federalist electors split theirs among John Eager Howard (22), James Ross (5), John Marshall (4) and Robert Goodloe Harper (3), and 4 votes were blank.","Rufus King was not formally nominated and did not campaign. His 34 electoral votes came from unpledged Federalist electors chosen by the legislatures of Connecticut, Delaware and Massachusetts.","The 17,300 votes for Federalist elector slates appear as Federalist “Unpledged electors,” so King has no popular vote of his own. In Pennsylvania an Independent ticket backed by disaffected Democratic-Republicans and Federalists received 17,597 votes and appears as Democratic-Republican “Unpledged electors.”","Returns are lost for 1 of Kentucky’s 3 districts and all 8 of Tennessee’s, so the popular vote is incomplete."],"winner":"monroe"},"congress":{"number":15,"years":"3/4/1817–3/3/1819","house":{"total":185,"seats":[["Democratic-Republicans",146],["Federalists",39]],"vacancies":0},"senate":{"total":38,"seats":[["Democratic-Republicans",25],["Federalists",13]],"vacancies":0}},"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia, \"1816 United States presidential election\"","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1816_United_States_presidential_election","rev":1385292976},{"title":"House Office of the Historian, \"Party Divisions of the House of Representatives,